for x in chosen:lines.append(f"| {x['contest_id']} | {x['displayed_entrant_label']} | {x['loaded_entries']} | {x['puka_CPT_allen_stafford_displayed_core_entries']} | {x['displayed_lineup_variants']} |")
lines+=['','omghedidnot’s rank653 roster kept Puka CPT / Allen / Stafford / Higbee / Corum and changed Ty Johnson to Colby Parkinson. That is direct evidence of a near-core variation inside the loaded portfolio; it does not tell us why the entrant built it. kingdom111 used a winning Puka CPT branch alongside Allen CPT, Rams DST CPT and Kyren CPT branches. Those are different scripts, not evidence that the entrant foresaw the winning script with certainty.','','What football intelligence could plausibly explain: a receiving Captain with his QB keeps the same passing scores together; a second QB supports two-sided scoring; inexpensive complementary pieces can pay for the expensive core. Opportunity cost is central: Corum and Ty Johnson may allow a stronger core than another expensive receiver. But route/carry forecast, teammate injury, salary discount and ownership claims need demonstrably pre-lock sources for this exact game. Current Sunday salaries/usage and later reconstructed research are not adequate substitutes. No winner submission-time rationale, generator provenance or actual pregame forecast from these entrants is available.','','Luck versus skill cannot be apportioned from this one result. A sound correlated core can still require rare touchdowns, substitute involvement and overtime; many variants increase coverage without establishing that any one forecast was more accurate. The record demonstrates repeated discovery of the same realized winner and differing portfolio breadth. It does not demonstrate private intelligence, total season skill, collusion, account ownership or calibrated probability.','','Sunday application: seek an opportunity story that changes the affordable correlated core, then preserve the plausible alternatives. Examples: Arizona’s post-Harrison receiving concentration changes which bring-back deserves salary; Buffalo’s diffuse receiving tree argues for testing one receiver rather than assuming double stack; Philadelphia replacement skill roles and Carolina cornerback IR create a question about which low-cost piece unlocks a strong core, but neither salary nor absences alone answer it. Measure actual pregame role/price evidence before transferring touches; compare branches on the same candidate pool; evaluate enough prospective decisions to distinguish repeated mechanism quality from one fortunate result.','','Source captures: monday_contests_capture.json, monday_winners_capture.json, monday_portfolio_summary.json. Entrant grouping removes only a trailing numeric entry suffix. Winner entry IDs/native file IDs and each observed roster are retained independently.']
text='\n'.join(lines)+'\n'
